"Hamiltonian cycles and 1-factors in 5-regular graphs (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0095895621001131)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Theorem 1 implies that there exist infinitely many 5-connected quintic graphs that have at least two Kempe equivalence classes. ... Conjecture ... Furthermore, the tetrahedron and the cube have one Kempe equivalence class, the octahedron and the icosahedron have two Kempe equivalence classes, and the dodecahedron has ten Kempe equivalence classes.\n\nCorollary 2\n\nLet G be a regular graph and F a non-perfect 1-factorisation of G. Then G admits no perfect 1-factorisation ${F}^{\\prime }\\sim F$. Thus, every planar quintic perfectly hamiltonian graph G on n vertices containing a 2-factor \\mathfrak{f}$\\mathfrak{f}$ composed of a 4-cycle and an $(n-4)$-cycle such that $G-E(\\mathfrak{f})$ is bridgeless, has at least two Kempe equivalence classes. In particular, planar 5-connected quintic strongly charonian graphs that are perfectly hamiltonian have at least two Kempe equivalence classes.\n\nProof\n\nIf we Kempe switch a hamiltonian cycle the number of perfect pairs remains unchanged: we are simply permuting the two colours of the edges of which the hamiltonian cycle is made. As F is non-perfect there exist colours 1 and 2 such that ${E}_{1}\\cup {E}_{2}$ is a non-hamiltonian 2-factor. Kempe switching a (necessarily non-hamiltonian) 1-2-coloured cycle cannot render ${E}_{1}\\cup {E}_{2}$ hamiltonian.\n\nBy Theorem 7 a planar 5-connected quintic strongly charonian graph G has a 1-factorisation containing at most nine perfect pairs. Therefore, if G is perfectly hamiltonian, it must have at least two Kempe equivalence classes. □\n\nTheorem 1 implies that there exist infinitely many 5-connected quintic graphs that have at least two Kempe equivalence classes. In fact, we believe the following to be true.\n\nConjecture\n\nEvery quintic graph contains at least two Kempe equivalence classes.\n\nA smallest planar counterexample would be cyclically 6-edge-connected and have at least 26 vertices.\n\nWe now enumerate perfect pairs and Kempe equivalence classes in the 1-skeleta of the Platonic solids. These results have, partially, already appeared in the literature. In the statements below, all counts are exact (and not lower bounds).\n\nProposition 3\n\nThe unique 1-factorisation of the tetrahedron contains only perfect pairs; the octahedron has two 1-factorisations which are each others mirror image and contain only perfect pairs; the cube has four 1-factorisations of which three have two perfect pairs and one has zero perfect pairs; all ten 1-factorisations of the dodecahedron have only perfect pairs; and there exist 1-factorisations of the icosahedron with k perfect pairs iff $k\\in \\{0,2,\\dots ,8\\}$. Furthermore, the tetrahedron and the cube have one Kempe equivalence class, the octahedron and the icosahedron have two Kempe equivalence classes, and the dodecahedron has ten Kempe equivalence classes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAmended Loneliness Spectrum Conjecture (https://www.emergentmind.com/open-problems/amended-loneliness-spectrum-conjecture)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; — The Lonely Runner Conjecture turns 60  (2409.20160 - Perarnau et al., 2024) in Conjecture 28, Section 10.2 (Spectrum of the loneliness gap)\n\nWe state the absence of a counterexample below $110$ as a conjecture (Conjecture \\ref{conj:parity}).\n\n# Amended Loneliness Spectrum Conjecture\n\nDetermine whether for every integer n ≥ 1 and every n-set V of positive integers, either κ(V) equals s/(sn + k) for some s,k ∈ N (almost tight instances), or κ(V) ≥ 1/n (uniform lower bound away from almost tight values).\n\n### Background\n\nKravitz originally conjectured a discrete spectrum for possible loneliness gaps, which was later disproved for some n. Fan and Sun proposed an amended conjecture that